Failing case: Which region was strongest in 2023 Q1?
  8 passages, 1,746 characters, wrong in at least 2 of 3 runs

  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 7 left
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 6 left
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 5 left
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 4 left
  dropping a passage fixes it           -> that one matters
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 3 left
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 2 left
  dropped a passage and it still fails  -> 1 left

  8 passages -> 1   1,746 -> 220 characters (87% smaller)

  what is left:

    ### Commentary Southwest remained our strongest region at $2,607,9

That is delta debugging, and the version above is the crude one — drop each
part, keep the drop if the bug survives. It is O(n) calls and good enough for
a context window; the published algorithm bisects and is worth reading when n
is large.

Two things make it work on a system like this one.

A test that is a *rate*, not a boolean. `fails()` samples three times and asks
for two, because a single run proves nothing — §24.1. Shrink against a flaky
predicate and the algorithm happily removes the thing that was causing the bug.

And shrinking the input, not the system. It is tempting to start disabling
features; that finds which component is involved, which you already knew. The
minimal input tells you what the component cannot handle, which is the thing
you have to fix.
